+# Tool Input Validation
+
+| Field  | Value                |
+| ------ | -------------------- |
+| Issue  | re-cinq/HALEngine#37 |
+| Status | In Progress          |
+
+Every tool declares a JSON Schema as its `inputSchema`, and that schema is handed verbatim to the providers to shape what the model sends. This spec makes the same schema the contract the engine enforces: `ToolRegistry.execute` validates a tool call's input against it before the executor runs, so a malformed call fails in one predictable way that the model can read and correct, rather than in a different way inside every executor.
+
+## Validation in `ToolRegistry.execute`
+
+- Input that omits a property the schema marks `required` is not passed to the executor; `execute` returns a `ToolResponse` whose `result` names the missing property as required, and the executor is never invoked ([validated by: returns a rejection naming the missing required property and never runs the executor](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L48)).
+- A property of the wrong declared type is rejected with a message naming the property path and the expected type, and neither the returned `result` nor any log line carries a value from the rejected input, because tool input is where identifying data enters the engine ([validated by: rejects a wrong-typed field by path and expected type without echoing any input value to the result or the log](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L62)).
+- A static tool's schema is compiled when it is registered, so `register` throws for a schema that cannot be compiled, with a message naming the tool, and a broken schema fails at startup rather than mid-conversation ([validated by: throws at register time for an uncompilable inputSchema, naming the tool](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L81)).
+- `execute` still throws `Unknown tool: <name>` for a name that was never registered; only input the schema rejects becomes a returned `ToolResponse`.
+
+## Rejection inside the tool loop
+
+- A rejection is a tool result, not an exception: the orchestrator feeds the rejection text back to the provider on the next round, a corrected retry runs the executor with the corrected input, and `processMessage` resolves rather than rejects ([validated by: feeds the rejection back to the provider and resolves after a corrected retry](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L103)).
